1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.500
Okay, let's dive into this.

2
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.432
Welcome back everyone to another learning
curve explainer, crafted specifically for

3
00:00:06.432 --> 00:00:07.500
the focused learner.

4
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.500
Today, we are tackling learning objective
11, conflict resolution.

5
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.954
As you well know, bringing a bunch of
people together to achieve a really

6
00:00:15.954 --> 00:00:18.500
complex goal is rarely a perfectly smooth
ride.

7
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:22.203
But by the end of this session, you're
going to be equipped to not just survive

8
00:00:22.203 --> 00:00:24.500
team friction, but actively manage it like
a pro.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.500
Here is our quick roadmap for today.

10
00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:29.500
One, introduction to conflict.

11
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.500
Two, sources across the project life
cycle.

12
00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.000
Three, the two sides of conflict.

13
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.500
Four, the Thomas-Kilmann instrument.

14
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.500
And five, core resolution principles.

15
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.500
Section one, introduction to conflict.

16
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:45.000
What is it really?

17
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:48.709
Now, you might be wondering, is your
project absolutely doomed the second

18
00:00:48.709 --> 00:00:49.500
someone disagrees?

19
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.991
It is such a common myth that all friction
in a team is a sign of ultimate failure

20
00:00:53.991 --> 00:00:55.500
or just really poor management.

21
00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.000
But I want to challenge that preconceived
notion right out of the gate.

22
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:02.500
Actually, disagreement doesn't mean your
project is failing.

23
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.000
In fact, it means your project involves
real people doing real complex work.

24
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.045
At its core, conflict is simply what
happens when stakeholders have differing,

25
00:01:11.045 --> 00:01:13.500
opposing attitudes that just aren't easily
reconciled.

26
00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.000
And with diverse teams, that's completely
natural.

27
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:21.226
Moving right along to section two, sources
across the project life cycle,

28
00:01:21.226 --> 00:01:22.500
anticipating friction.

29
00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:27.683
Now, what's really interesting here is
that conflict isn't just random, it is an

30
00:01:27.683 --> 00:01:31.500
unavoidable reality baked into literally
every single phase of a project.

31
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.500
Let's walk through this timeline together.

32
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.465
In the initial concept phase, you know,
you're usually battling over securing that

33
00:01:37.465 --> 00:01:40.000
initial funding and just getting the
business case approved.

34
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.500
Then, moving into definition, the tension
shifts.

35
00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:46.827
Now you're trying to align that scope to a
strict budget, you're arguing over

36
00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:50.327
resource plans, and basically fighting
over who gets the last slice of the budget

37
00:01:50.327 --> 00:01:50.500
pie.

38
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
And then in deployment, wow, things get
very real.

39
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:58.419
Conflict here usually comes from missed
critical deadlines, resource availability

40
00:01:58.419 --> 00:02:02.000
suddenly drying up, or team members simply
not following agreed processes.

41
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.187
As you reach transition, you'll start
seeing debates over whether acceptance

42
00:02:06.187 --> 00:02:09.000
criteria and end-user expectations have
actually been met.

43
00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.766
During adoption, the friction is all about
whether the business's usual teams are

44
00:02:12.766 --> 00:02:14.500
truly adopting the outputs as you
intended.

45
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.701
And finally, in benefits realisation, you
might find stakeholders actively debating

46
00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:22.000
the final measurements and whether that
promised value was truly realised.

47
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:28.135
So, if conflict is absolutely inevitable
at every single stage from concepts to

48
00:02:28.135 --> 00:02:29.500
benefits realisation,

49
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.500
does that mean we're just doomed to a
miserable experience?

50
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:33.500
Not at all.

51
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:38.056
And that brings us to a really crucial
pivot in how we need to view team

52
00:02:38.056 --> 00:02:38.500
dynamics.

53
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.000
Section three, the two sides of conflict,
positive and negative impacts.

54
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.500
We really need to completely reframe how
we look at friction.

55
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:50.000
Because it actually has this wild dual
nature.

56
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.000
Let's look at the negative side first.

57
00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.293
The dark side of conflict is that if you
leave it unresolved, it derails your

58
00:02:56.293 --> 00:03:00.382
objectives, it creates awful interpersonal
friction, it lowers staff morale, and it

59
00:03:00.382 --> 00:03:02.500
wastes incredibly valuable management
time.

60
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.000
But, look at the positive side.

61
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:08.000
Conflict is actually an amazing tool for
adding value.

62
00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:11.500
Think about it, it forces open
conversations and really lively debate.

63
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:15.182
It allows people to express their true
feelings and reveals those hidden agendas,

64
00:03:15.182 --> 00:03:17.500
so you know exactly where your
stakeholders actually stand.

65
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.797
Ultimately, hashing out a disagreement
enables crystal clear clarity of direction

66
00:03:21.797 --> 00:03:22.500
moving forward.

67
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:26.000
But here is the absolute most crucial
point.

68
00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:30.500
Ignoring conflict is never a productive
strategy for success.

69
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.172
I mean, sticking your head in the sand,
that's literally the only guaranteed way

70
00:03:35.172 --> 00:03:39.031
to take a potentially productive debate
and turn it into a project-killing

71
00:03:39.031 --> 00:03:39.500
disaster.

72
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.000
The friction isn't just going to magically
go away.

73
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:44.000
We have to address it head-on.

74
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.809
Let's move to section four and see how
this builds into our next tool, the

75
00:03:48.809 --> 00:03:52.000
Thomas-Kilmann instrument, or TKI, a
framework for resolution.

76
00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.778
The TKI is arguably your ultimate tactical
toolkit for knowing exactly how to

77
00:03:56.778 --> 00:03:59.500
address project disputes in different
situations.

78
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.000
It encourages you to evaluate conflict
along two distinct dimensions.

79
00:04:04.500 --> 00:04:07.703
Up the vertical axis, we have
assertiveness, which is basically your

80
00:04:07.703 --> 00:04:11.000
drive to meet your own personal needs and
achieve your own objectives.

81
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:14.729
And across the horizontal axis is
cooperativeness, your desire to meet the

82
00:04:14.729 --> 00:04:17.500
needs of others and help them achieve
their objectives.

83
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:22.108
When we map out high and low levels of
these two drives, we end up with five

84
00:04:22.108 --> 00:04:24.000
distinct conflict resolution modes.

85
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:25.000
Let's break them down.

86
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:29.000
First up, top left corner of high
assertiveness and low cooperativeness.

87
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Compete.

88
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.000
This is your I win, you lose mode.

89
00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:36.019
Now, competing gets a bad rap, right? But
it is perfectly valid and entirely

90
00:04:36.019 --> 00:04:37.500
necessary in certain situations.

91
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.435
When there's a clear right or wrong
answer, an emergency pops up, or an

92
00:04:41.435 --> 00:04:45.112
unpopular decision just has to be made
quickly, taking decisive, competitive

93
00:04:45.112 --> 00:04:47.000
action is vital to protect the project.

94
00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:50.652
Think of a safety hazard on site, you
don't sit around and debate it, you stop

95
00:04:50.652 --> 00:04:51.500
the work immediately.

96
00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.000
Dropping down to low assertiveness and low
cooperativeness, we've got avoid.

97
00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.500
The I lose, you lose mode.

98
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:02.500
You might be thinking, why on earth would
I ever choose to lose?

99
00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:06.778
Well, intentionally side-stepping and
avert disagreement is actually a highly

100
00:05:06.778 --> 00:05:11.278
smart tactic when the issue is totally
trivial, when you just need time to gather

101
00:05:11.278 --> 00:05:15.722
more information, or when the potential
damage and disruption of a massive fight

102
00:05:15.722 --> 00:05:18.500
drastically outweighs the benefit of
resolving it.

103
00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:21.000
You've got to save your energy for the big
battles.

104
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:24.000
Right smack in the centre is compromise.

105
00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.000
This is the we both half win and half lose
mode.

106
00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.500
It represents moderate assertiveness and
moderate cooperativeness.

107
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.500
Compromise is your practical middle
ground.

108
00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.078
You use this when the goals are definitely
important, but you are under severe time

109
00:05:36.078 --> 00:05:37.500
pressure to find a solution fast.

110
00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.293
Through negotiation, mutual concessions
are made to create a temporary, workable

111
00:05:41.293 --> 00:05:43.000
settlement to really complex issues.

112
00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.500
No one gets exactly what they want, but
hey, the project keeps moving.

113
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.000
Up in the top right corner, we find
collaborate.

114
00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:51.000
This is the gold standard, folks.

115
00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:54.000
High assertiveness paired with high
cooperativeness.

116
00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:56.500
The I win, you win mode.

117
00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:00.350
It involves genuinely sharing diverse
perspectives and information to reach a

118
00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:03.500
true consensus where everyone's core
concerns are incorporated.

119
00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:08.286
Imagine trying to merge two completely
different department workflows into one

120
00:06:08.286 --> 00:06:09.000
new software.

121
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.000
You absolutely have to collaborate to
ensure everyone can still do their jobs.

122
00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:16.500
But a quick warning, collaboration is
highly time-intensive.

123
00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.915
You want to use this when the objective is
just too important to compromise on, and

124
00:06:20.915 --> 00:06:24.500
when developing a really strong long-term
working relationship is paramount.

125
00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.000
Finally, bottom right corner, accommodate.

126
00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.500
Low assertiveness, high cooperativeness.

127
00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.500
The I lose, you win mode.

128
00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:37.179
This is a highly strategic choice to
actively prioritize team harmony and

129
00:06:37.179 --> 00:06:40.500
relationship stability over winning the
specific argument.

130
00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.868
You use accommodation when you suddenly
realise you are actually in the wrong,

131
00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:48.637
when the issue is simply way more
important to the other person, or when you

132
00:06:48.637 --> 00:06:52.703
strategically want to build up some social
credits for a later, much more critical

133
00:06:52.703 --> 00:06:53.000
issue.

134
00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:58.500
Section five, core resolution principles,
managing conflict in practice.

135
00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.500
So, how do we actually manage this in the
real world?

136
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.500
There are six core steps to keep in your
back pocket.

137
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:06.500
One, anticipate friction.

138
00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.979
Just assume people will have different
agendas, it really shouldn't surprise you

139
00:07:09.979 --> 00:07:10.500
at this point.

140
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.500
Two, acknowledge the issue.

141
00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:14.500
Conflict rarely just disappears on its
own.

142
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.500
Three, resolve without blame.

143
00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:21.104
You have to focus entirely on a solution
that benefits the project, not on pointing

144
00:07:21.104 --> 00:07:21.500
fingers.

145
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.500
Four, encourage discussion.

146
00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.000
Honest dialogue allows people to actually
see each other's perspectives.

147
00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:29.000
Five, facilitate collaboration.

148
00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:32.500
Ask questions to help the team explore
potential resolutions themselves.

149
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:36.000
And six, and this is perhaps the most
critical one, remain neutral.

150
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.000
Use a neutral tone, neutral language, and
above all, neutral body language.

151
00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:43.604
Stripping away that defensiveness from
heated project meetings is literally the

152
00:07:43.604 --> 00:07:46.000
fastest way to get everyone back to
productive communication.

153
00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:52.020
But just as a quick pragmatic reality
check here, sometimes local facilitation

154
00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.500
just straight up fails.

155
00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:57.660
And if you cannot resolve the conflict
within the immediate team, you absolutely

156
00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:00.000
must rely on the project's formal
governance.

157
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.196
Clear protocols and the involvement of
external parties might be entirely

158
00:08:04.196 --> 00:08:08.044
required to prevent the situation from
escalating into a total unrecoverable

159
00:08:08.044 --> 00:08:08.500
meltdown.

160
00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:11.000
You have to know when to escalate.

161
00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:15.084
We've covered a tremendous amount of
ground today, from predicting the sources

162
00:08:15.084 --> 00:08:18.852
of project conflict to embracing its dual
nature, all the way through applying the

163
00:08:18.852 --> 00:08:20.000
Thomas-Kilmann framework.

164
00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:23.500
So I want to leave you with one final
provocative thought to chew on.

165
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.000
Look inward for a second.

166
00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.500
Which of those five TKI modes is your
natural default?

167
00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.500
Are you always compromising, always
competing?

168
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.716
And is that default behaviour secretly
holding your project back from reaching

169
00:08:34.716 --> 00:08:35.500
its full potential?

170
00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:40.071
Analysing your own behaviour is the very
first step to truly mastering team

171
00:08:40.071 --> 00:08:40.500
dynamics.

172
00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:44.000
Thanks for focusing in with us today, and
I'll catch you in the next explainer.
